WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.010
- Legal Eagle is coming in loud and clear

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:03.363
thanks to Ting Mobile.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.303
Today we're talking about

00:00:05.303 --> 00:00:07.553
♪ Oklahoma ♪

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.190
- Where the wind doesn't
come sweeping down the plain

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:14.110
and the waving wheat does not smell sweet

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:17.730
because it appears that
Oklahoma is no more.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.500
Is it possible that the Supreme Court

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.960
gave away half of the state?

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.800
Well, in what some people
are calling legal insanity,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.780
the Supreme Court in the immigrant cases

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:29.850
decided that Native-Americans
still have reservations

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:34.850
in most of Eastern Oklahoma in
an area that includes Tulsa.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.140
The Supreme Court says that
Native-American reservations

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.330
have jurisdiction over some of the crimes

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:43.780
that happen in that huge
swath of Eastern Oklahoma.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.590
And Justice Roberts is warning that

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.210
this is just the tip of the iceberg,

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:50.420
that perhaps all of the
laws may eventually fall

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.000
under the reservation jurisdiction

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.820
and no longer be part of Oklahoma.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.410
So the question is,

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:59.476
did the Supreme Court just
give away half of Oklahoma?

00:00:59.476 --> 00:01:01.893
(soft music)

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:03.813
Hey Legal Eagles.

00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:05.120
It's time to think like a lawyer.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.650
Because if you live in Tulsa, Oklahoma,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.010
you might be wondering whose
laws you have to follow

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.510
and who you have to pay your taxes to.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:15.190
But some of the more fundamental questions

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:17.620
are what is a Native-American reservation?

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.150
And how does that impact
the laws that apply

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.360
in a given geographical area?

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.380
And does Eastern Oklahoma
belong to the state of Oklahoma

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.880
or other Native-American reservations?

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:34.080
Or did Oklahoma ever in fact
exist as a state itself?

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.020
(indistinct)

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.220
Well, the bombshell decision
of McGirt versus Oklahoma,

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.240
delves into the interplay
between state law

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.040
and the validity of
land rights established

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.620
by treaties granted by the US Government.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.010
With some, including
the descent contending

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.670
that this decision will
upend governance in Oklahoma.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:54.310
And I've had many people ask
what this case is really about.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:55.790
And you would think that a decision

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.197
that begins with the words

00:01:57.197 --> 00:02:00.460
"On the far end of the trail
of tears was a promise"

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.350
will surely find its way into
case law books in the future.

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:06.060
But this was a five, four decision

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.160
that's even more interesting

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.040
because the author of
the majority opinion,

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.370
Justice Neil Gorsuch broke
with his right leaning cohorts

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:13.410
on the Supreme Court.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.330
And of course it gets
even more interesting

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.970
with multiple descents
with Clarence Thomas

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.290
writing his own descent

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:20.340
because more often than not,

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.920
Thomas can't even agree with
the majority of the minority

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:23.760
in its entirety.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:24.630
So without further ado,

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.090
let's break down the incredible case

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:27.840
of McGirt versus the state of Oklahoma

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.390
and find out what's going on here.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:31.460
So this case starts with the conviction

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.710
of one named Jimcy McGirt.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:37.140
Which of course is the most
Oklahoma name you've ever heard

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.060
in your entire life.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.360
And McGirt was found guilty
of three sexual offenses

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.010
and sentenced to life in prison in 1996.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.200
He challenged his conviction

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.140
on the basis that the Oklahoma State Court

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:48.970
lacked jurisdiction to prosecute him

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.860
because he's an enrolled member

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.890
of the Seminole nation of Oklahoma

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.550
and his crimes took place on
designated reservation land.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.650
And if indeed that's
true that McGirt's crimes

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.870
took place on reservation land.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:02.990
Then he should have been
prosecuted in Federal Court,

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.810
not Oklahoma State Court
under the Major Crimes Act.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:06.740
The deciding factor

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:08.367
for purposes of the Supreme Court analysis

00:03:08.367 --> 00:03:11.070
was whether the land
given to Native-Americans

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.000
and treaties by the US government,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.590
remains an Indian reservation

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:16.730
for purposes of federal criminal law.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:18.750
Now, before we dive into
the specific arguments

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.070
by both the petitioner
and the state of Oklahoma,

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:23.760
I think it's important to
provide some history here

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.360
because that backdrop
informs this particular case

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.330
and explains why these treaties
existed in the first place.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.100
Most remember in middle
school history class,

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.240
learning about what is
commonly referred to as

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:36.430
the "Trail of Tears"

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:38.220
And here's a quick refresher.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.330
The 1830s Native-Americans
occupied large tracks of land

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.920
in states such as Georgia,
Tennessee, North Carolina,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.060
Alabama, and Florida.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.180
They resided on this land for generations,

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:48.400
of course, long before Columbus,

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:49.980
the Pilgrims or the Mayflower

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.930
because of the viability of this land,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.670
white settlers wanted to
use their land for farming.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:55.890
So in 1830,

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.720
president Andrew Jackson
signed the Indian Removal Act,

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.580
which gave the federal
government authority

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.100
to exchange this land
held by Native-Americans

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:08.010
for land out West in an
Indian colonization zone,

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.000
which is now present day, Oklahoma.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.390
Faced with an invasion
by the US government

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:13.650
and all of the atrocities

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.710
that we know go hand in hand with that

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:17.760
the Native-Americans
left behind their homes,

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.585
which were alluded while they
were forced to journey on foot

00:04:20.585 --> 00:04:22.670
to the land in Oklahoma,

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:24.860
without any supplies or
assistance from the government

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:25.810
that was kicking them out.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:27.860
Thousands of Native-Americans
lost their lives

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.540
on this forced expedition,

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:32.387
which is why one tribal leader
accurately referred to it as

00:04:32.387 --> 00:04:34.730
"a trail of tears and death."

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:37.850
4,000 people died on the trail of tears.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:39.820
Now, while the main issues involved,

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:41.390
whether there is a reservation

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:43.780
or if it was just established by Congress

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.060
and what that means.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.140
It's important to know
why this determination

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:48.930
has such a significant impact.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:52.150
In 1885, Congress passed
the Major Crimes Act,

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:55.170
which places certain crimes
committed by Native-Americans

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:56.970
in Native-American territory

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.990
as subject to federal
not state jurisdiction.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:03.150
It states that within quote
"the "Indian Country",

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.930
any Indian who committed
certain enumerated offenses

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.430
against the person or
property of another Indian

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:09.263
or any of that person

00:05:09.263 --> 00:05:11.557
shall be subject to the same law penalties

00:05:11.557 --> 00:05:14.787
as all other persons committing
any of the above offenses

00:05:14.787 --> 00:05:17.600
within the exclusive jurisdiction
of the United States."

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.307
The MCA defines quote "Indian Country" as

00:05:20.307 --> 00:05:23.290
"all land within the limits
of any Indian reservation

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:25.420
under the jurisdiction of
the United States government,

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:27.320
not withstanding the
issuance of any patent

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:30.180
and including rights of way
running through the reservation.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.140
So the determination of
reservation or no reservation

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.360
is the difference between
some crimes being adjudicated

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:36.580
by the federal court,

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:37.900
as opposed to the state court.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:39.120
So let's turn to the issues

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.120
that were at issue in
McGirt versus Oklahoma.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.280
The first question is
did Congress establish

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.690
a Native-American reservation
in the first place?

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:48.580
If there was no reservation established,

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:51.210
then the petitioner's entire
argument would be moot.

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:55.090
State law, not the federal MCA
would be the controlling law.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:57.960
The majority opinion concluded
that McGirt was right.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.480
That Congress did establish a reservation

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.300
for the land in question.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.510
Large swaths of Oklahoma.

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:05.570
To arrive at this conclusion,

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:08.260
the court had to scrutinize
the original treaty

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:10.790
with the group known as the Creek Nation

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:13.990
that was ratified and
enacted into law in 1833.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:16.640
The treaty in no uncertain terms declared

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.330
the parcel of land that they were giving

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:20.780
in what is now located within Oklahoma

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:22.530
to be a quote "a permanent home

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.580
to the whole Creek Nation of Indians."

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:27.230
The treaty even spells out how
they would accomplish this.

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:29.627
The United States, it
provides would quote,

00:06:29.627 --> 00:06:31.640
"grant a patent in fee simple

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:34.780
to the Creek Nation of Indians
for the assigned land."

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:37.780
To continue, "so long as
they shall exist as a nation

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:39.760
and continue to occupied the country

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:41.050
hereby assigned to them."

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:43.410
Within these lands, the
creeks were to be secured

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.410
in their unrestricted
right of self-governance

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:48.710
with full jurisdiction over
all enrolled tribe members

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:49.770
and their property.

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:53.680
This treaty, though many
Oklahomans aren't happy about it

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:55.810
is not really ambiguous.

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:59.100
It clearly defines the
large swaths of area

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.900
that fall under this treaty

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.330
and become a Native-American reservation.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:04.260
So the next question is,

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:07.130
does the reservation still exist today

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:08.980
or has it been disestablished

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:10.980
by a subsequent congressional acts?

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:12.790
And the majority opinion
notes that over the years,

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:15.170
Congress has violated
its treaty obligations.

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:17.100
And this initially undivided land

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.330
is now fractured into various portions,

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:22.030
held by tribe members and
unaffiliated members alike.

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:25.089
Nevertheless, the court
opinion notes rather strongly

00:07:25.089 --> 00:07:27.160
that the federal treaties and statutes

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:29.370
are the Supreme law of the land

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:31.700
and that it's up to
Congress and Congress alone

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:34.310
to decide if it wants to
breach those treaties.

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:35.970
The point being that only Congress,

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.520
not the states and certainly
not the judicial branch

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:40.860
can explicitly disestablish
the reservation.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.060
This reminder of the importance
of separation of powers

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:44.550
among the three branches of government

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.700
has been flashed out
in countless opinions,

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:49.477
as well as Saturday morning
episodes of Schoolhouse Rock.

00:07:49.477 --> 00:07:52.458
♪ Ring one, Executive ♪

00:07:52.458 --> 00:07:56.417
♪ Two is Legislative, that's Congress ♪

00:07:56.417 --> 00:07:59.247
♪ Ring three, Judiciary ♪

00:07:59.247 --> 00:08:03.234
♪ See it's kind of like my circus ♪

00:08:03.234 --> 00:08:04.830
♪ My circus. ♪

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:05.960
- But in writing the opinion

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:07.770
for the majority Justice Gorsuch,

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:09.477
summed it up pretty well saying that,

00:08:09.477 --> 00:08:12.410
"Legislators might seek
to pass laws that tiptoe

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:13.820
to the edge of disestablishment

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.890
and hope that judges facing no possibility

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:17.690
of electoral consequences themselves

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:19.080
will deliver the final push,

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.110
but wishes don't make for laws

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:22.730
and saving the political branches

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.390
the embarrassment of
disestablishing a reservation

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:28.320
is not one of our constitutionally
assigned prerogatives."

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:30.720
Recall the Gorsuch is a textualist.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:33.830
He looks to the text of the various laws,

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:35.540
and while he might not agree

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:37.310
with what the law says politically,

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:40.740
if he thinks that the text
militates a certain outcome,

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.070
that's the way that he is going to vote

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:43.903
on the Supreme Court.

00:08:43.903 --> 00:08:46.610
And the bottom line is
that this is on Congress.

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:49.180
And if they want to
disestablish the reservation,

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:50.452
they need to do it explicitly

00:08:50.452 --> 00:08:52.980
without getting the courts
to do its dirty work.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:55.280
But that's not the end of the inquiry.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:56.274
Because the question remains,

00:08:56.274 --> 00:08:59.450
did some sort of
subsequent act of Congress

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:02.047
actually disestablished the reservation?

00:09:02.047 --> 00:09:04.190
Oklahoma actually claimed that Congress

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:06.490
did disestablish the land
within the reservation,

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:08.280
starting around around the 1880s.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.100
As a result of the rationally
authorized Dawes Commission,

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:13.260
the tribes were pressured to abandon

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:14.850
their customary communal living

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:18.740
and issue out individual
tracks of lands to its members.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:21.570
Both the General Allotment Act of 1887

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:24.410
and the Creek Act of 1901
encouraged Creek members

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:25.830
to divide up their lands.

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:28.759
This eventually resulted in
some individual tribe members

00:09:28.759 --> 00:09:31.710
selling their land to non
Native-Americans as well.

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:32.950
But according to the majority,

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:35.040
this was nothing like an
actual act of Congress

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.510
disestablishing the entire reservation.

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:40.640
Prior Supreme Court cases
already had held that allotment

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:42.260
is completely consistent

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:44.410
with the continued reservation status.

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:45.830
Moreover, as we talked about

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:47.130
the language of the treaty itself

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:49.687
also made this clear by
defining "Indian Country" as

00:09:49.687 --> 00:09:51.890
"all land within the limits
of any Indian reservation,

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.070
not withstanding the
issuance of any patents,

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:55.530
and including any rights-of-way running

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:56.440
through the reservation."

00:09:56.440 --> 00:09:58.320
In other words, the treaty
provides for the land

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.770
to be encumbered and divided in a way

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:03.640
that doesn't affect the
overall reservation itself.

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:05.030
And to drive the point home,

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:06.480
the court provides an analogy

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:08.980
that shows that fallacy
of equating allotment.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:09.813
In other words,

00:10:09.813 --> 00:10:12.190
dividing up the land to disestablish it,

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:14.540
getting rid of the reservation entirely.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:17.090
The federal government has
issued its own allotment

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:18.690
to the form of what are called patents

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:22.410
that transfer legal title
to homesteads in the West.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:24.010
And no one is going to argue that

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:26.030
the United States
sovereignty over the land

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:28.780
that they gave these homestead patents to

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:32.240
is in any way diminished because
of these particular acts.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:33.320
And the same holds true

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:35.400
for the land buying the Creek Nation.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.000
The plan might have been
to go from allotment

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:40.168
to disestablishment of the reservation,

00:10:40.168 --> 00:10:43.610
but Congress never followed through.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:46.420
And so the reservation still exists.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:48.520
And although Congress took other actions

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:50.840
that impacted the Creek
Nation's reservation status,

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:52.810
like abolishing their tribal courts

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:54.760
and transferring all
of their pending civil

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:56.990
and criminal cases to
United States courts,

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:58.990
the Supreme Court has held
that none of these acts

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:01.340
disestablished the reservation itself,

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:04.410
even what's called the five
civilized tribes act of 1906,

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:07.670
which took away more of
the Native-American rights

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:10.220
stomped short of actually
disestablishing the reservation,

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:11.620
the Creek Nation still retained

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:12.810
their tribal government power

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:15.290
to do such things as collect taxes,

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:18.050
operate schools and legislate
through tribal ordinances.

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:19.620
However, arguably the strongest evidence

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.480
that the court provided was that in 1936,

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:22.891
Congress authorized the Creek Nation

00:11:22.891 --> 00:11:25.390
to adopt a constitution and bylaws,

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:26.820
which actually restored powers

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:29.360
that they had previously been deprived of.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:30.490
And to give you a little idea

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:31.930
of what the government now looks like,

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:33.900
it contains three separate
branches of government,

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:36.630
much like the US government generally,

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:39.040
a democratically elected principal chief,

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:40.770
its own police force, hospitals,

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:43.310
and an annual budget of 350 million.

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:44.240
So in other words,

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:45.880
the Creek Nation still exists.

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:50.310
And so does the reservation
in most of Eastern Oklahoma.

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:51.900
Now that said Oklahoma and the descent

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:55.080
trying to use circumstantial
and the land's demographics

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:57.000
as evidence of disestablishment.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:58.170
In defending their position,

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.250
Oklahoma suggested a three step process

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.212
to determine disestablishment
where the court,

00:12:02.212 --> 00:12:04.750
A, reviews the laws passed by Congress.

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:06.900
B, examines the contemporary events

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.690
at the time these laws were passed

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:11.880
and C evaluates subsequent
events and demographics.

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:13.030
In laying out this approach,

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:14.260
Oklahoma and the descent,

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:16.270
point out that state's ongoing practice

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:20.430
of prosecuting Native-Americans
located in "Indian Country"

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:21.570
for over a century,

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:24.320
as well as the massive
influx of white settlers

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:26.560
into the Creek Nations
lands over the years.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.470
And they also point to various statements

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:31.050
made during the allotment era,

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:32.420
including those from the Creek Nation.

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:34.930
That seemed to indicate that
most believed the reservation

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:37.260
was on his last leg and in the process

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:38.620
of being disestablished.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:41.440
But the majority's
response to this argument

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.860
is basically thanks
for the history lesson,

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:45.830
but that's not dispositive.

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:46.750
It doesn't matter.

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:49.576
Well, the court didn't deny
that the context can be useful.

00:12:49.576 --> 00:12:51.030
It's only useful if the court

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:53.440
is interpreting ambiguous statutory terms,

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:55.280
which is a common Canon of construction.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:57.530
When you're dealing with a language,

00:12:57.530 --> 00:12:59.710
whether it's a statute or a contract,

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:01.439
if you have a contract dispute

00:13:01.439 --> 00:13:05.020
and two parties want different things

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:06.150
and you look to the contract

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:08.270
and it's absolutely crystal clear,

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:10.530
then there's no reason to then think about

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:13.510
what the parties might have
said in negotiating the contract

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:15.620
because the contractual
language in this case

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:16.730
is perfectly clear.

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:18.580
But if the contract isn't clear,

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:19.640
then you're going to have to look at

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:21.040
what's called parole evidence,

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:23.580
evidence outside of the
four corners of the contract

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:24.700
to determine what to do.

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.650
And that's what the court is doing here.

00:13:26.650 --> 00:13:28.760
And as far as the court was concerned,

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:30.720
there weren't any ambiguous terms here.

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:34.260
The treaty might be old,
but the terms were clear.

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:35.700
And as the majority pointed out,

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.360
Oklahoma and the dissenting
justice identify any cases

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:40.320
where these kinds of
extratextual considerations

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.580
led to the disestablishment
of a reservation.

00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:44.770
So that takes us to Oklahoma's final

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:46.320
sort of last ditch argument,

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.710
which actually the dissenters
in this case seized upon.

00:13:48.710 --> 00:13:51.550
And it's what we attorneys
call a policy argument.

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:54.130
A policy argument is designed
to appeal to common sense

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:56.630
by highlighting the real world impact

00:13:56.630 --> 00:13:59.050
that a legal ruling could actually have.

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:01.010
These types of arguments rely more

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:02.810
on the factual implications of a ruling

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:05.070
rather than the actual statutory language

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:07.320
or constitutional framework.

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:08.670
And in this case,

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:10.210
the argument went something like this.

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:12.550
If the court decides that
the reservation exists today,

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:15.098
then one of two things
could potentially happen.

00:14:15.098 --> 00:14:17.960
One other similarly situated tribes,

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:19.390
pre-existing treaties,

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:22.160
will claim that their
reservations still exist

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:23.820
as well with all the rights and privileges

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:24.980
that go along with that.

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:26.044
Number two and more to the point,

00:14:26.044 --> 00:14:29.540
this decision would destroy
both the numerous convictions

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:30.710
that have already been doled out

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:33.540
as well as their ability
to prosecute future crimes

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:35.490
on Native-American reservation land.

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:36.600
And in the past,

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.880
these kinds of policy
arguments have actually worked

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:41.917
to shoot down Native-American
claims on their own land,

00:14:41.917 --> 00:14:44.000
but here are the majority
wasn't having it.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.510
First they point out
that the MCA is limited.

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:48.460
It only applies to certain types of crimes

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:50.900
and B it does not apply to cases involving

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:53.290
non Native-American victims or defendants.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.130
And the court also shoots
down on the claim that

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:56.870
the sky is falling by reminding us that

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:59.780
the population of the area
only consists of 10 to 15%

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:00.870
of Native-Americans,

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.000
and many convictions
would not be challenged

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:05.390
because of the limitations
on post conviction review

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:08.010
and a desire to not roll
the dice in federal court.

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:10.500
After all, just because you're
not supposed to be tried

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:12.290
in state court doesn't mean you get away.

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:14.390
It just means you're tried
in federal court instead.

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:17.890
And above all Justice Gorsuch
rejected histrionic claims

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:20.370
saying that we proceed
well aware of the potential

00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:23.010
for constant conflict around
jurisdictional boundaries,

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:26.200
especially ones that have gone
unappreciated for so long.

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:28.568
But it's unclear why
pessimism should rule the day.

00:15:28.568 --> 00:15:30.490
With the passage of time Oklahoma

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:31.760
and its triumphs have proven that

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:34.350
they can work successfully
together as partners.

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:35.700
And one of the things
that's really interesting

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:38.000
about this particular
case is you get a sampling

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:39.510
of the kinds of arguments

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:42.270
that you see in a lot of
different Supreme Court cases.

00:15:42.270 --> 00:15:44.810
Some argue from the original text,

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:48.430
some argue from the context
in which laws are passed

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:50.620
and some just flat out reject all that

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:53.040
and just argue from the policy arguments.

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:55.200
But it's interesting to see what worked

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.070
in this particular case and what didn't.

00:15:57.070 --> 00:16:00.330
But finally we get to why you
probably came to this video

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:01.460
in the first place.

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:03.220
What are the implications

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:05.910
of this potentially momentous case?

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:07.030
Well in general,

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:08.942
this is an important decision
because first of all,

00:16:08.942 --> 00:16:12.380
Gorsuch's opinion was
seen as an acknowledgement

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.150
that often Native-Americans

00:16:14.150 --> 00:16:16.720
have been promised many
things by Congress.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:20.180
And those promises have
generally not been held up

00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:22.759
and here Gorsuch and the majority

00:16:22.759 --> 00:16:25.410
are holding Congress's feet to the fire

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:28.420
and making them follow through

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:30.580
with what was originally promised,

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:33.760
even if it was promised over 100 years ago

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:36.010
or rather over 200 years ago.

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:38.880
And although Congress has
diminished and restricted

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:42.320
different tribes authority on
their reservations severely,

00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:44.180
the reservation still exists

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:45.610
until Congress says otherwise.

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:48.470
And of course, Congress can say otherwise.

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:51.820
And at the heart of this
battle is a fairly arcane law

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:54.650
that deals with simply
who has jurisdiction

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:56.830
to actually prosecute crimes.

00:16:56.830 --> 00:17:00.720
Here "the MCA applies to
Oklahoma on it' usual term saying

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:02.190
that only the federal government

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:04.660
and not the state may
prosecute Native-Americans

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:07.960
for crimes committed in Indian Country."

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:09.460
Of course, the larger question is

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:11.780
whether this decision
in the words of Ted Cruz

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:13.890
gives away half of Oklahoma.

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:16.220
But really the practical
implications of this decision

00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:17.250
are not that dramatic.

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:19.900
The case does not change
any land ownership

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:21.060
in Eastern Oklahoma.

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:22.620
Oklahomans are not going to be forced

00:17:22.620 --> 00:17:24.577
to abandon their homes
and walk a thousand miles

00:17:24.577 --> 00:17:25.960
in search of a new home.

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:27.680
The decision also does not grant

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:29.010
the tribe criminal jurisdiction

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:31.020
over Non-Native Americans.

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:33.550
But it does mean that state
prosecutors lack of authority

00:17:33.550 --> 00:17:36.700
to pursue criminal cases against
Native-American defendants

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:40.850
in parts of Oklahoma, that
includes most of Tulsa,

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:42.810
the state's second largest city.

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:43.643
But in fairness,

00:17:43.643 --> 00:17:45.250
there's a lot less unresolved.

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:47.980
The majority decision
left open the possibility

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:49.890
of other impacts between
territorial rights

00:17:49.890 --> 00:17:52.740
and taxation and other
jurisdictional issues.

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:56.000
But the court felt that Oklahoma

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:57.960
and the Native-American tribes

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:00.050
could resolve these things amicably.

00:18:00.050 --> 00:18:02.830
It's unlikely that anything
dramatic is going to happen.

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:03.663
After this decision,

00:18:03.663 --> 00:18:06.680
the state and five tribes
issued a joint statement,

00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:09.160
stating that "The nations and the state

00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:10.850
are committed to implementing a framework

00:18:10.850 --> 00:18:11.970
of shared jurisdiction

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:13.470
that will preserve sovereign interests

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:14.900
and rights to self-government

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:16.970
while affirming
jurisdictional understandings,

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:18.490
procedures, laws, and regulations

00:18:18.490 --> 00:18:20.530
that support public safety, our economy,

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:21.662
and private property rights.

00:18:21.662 --> 00:18:24.120
We will continue our work confident

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:25.972
that we can accomplish more together

00:18:25.972 --> 00:18:28.200
than any of us could alone."

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.520
So even if you live in Eastern Oklahoma,

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:32.340
it's unlikely that your day to day life

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:33.770
is going to be affected very much.

